## The strongest evidence against this thesis

This section comes before the supporting evidence, not after it.

**1. Real usage did not grow. It fell by {{ h.H1.trend.total_change_pct | abs | pct_direct }}.**
Economic accounts went from {{ h.H1.trend.first | num }} to
{{ h.H1.trend.last | num }} over the window, a slope of
{{ h.H1.trend.slope_per_month | signed }} accounts per month with a 95%
interval of [{{ h.H1.trend.ci_low | num }}, {{ h.H1.trend.ci_high | num }}].
The interval does not reach zero.

This is not an artefact of where we set the bar. Across the
{{ h.H1.grid_summary.cells }}-cell sensitivity grid the trend is **down in
{{ h.H1.grid_summary.down }} cells, flat in {{ h.H1.grid_summary.flat }}, and
up in {{ h.H1.grid_summary.up }}**. The grid label is
**{{ h.H1.robustness }}**. Tightening the filter makes the decline steeper,
which is the opposite of what a threshold artefact looks like.

**2. Activity concentrated sharply rather than broadening.**
The top-10 receiver share rose from {{ h.H2.series.top10_share[0].value | pct }}
to {{ h.H2.series.top10_share[-1].value | pct }}, and HHI rose from
{{ h.H2.series.hhi[0].value | num(4) }} to {{ h.H2.series.hhi[-1].value | num(4) }}.
We predicted both would fall.

**3. Fee burn does not come close to offsetting issuance, and is shrinking.**
In the final month, burn was {{ h.H5.materiality_bar.final_month_value | pct(2) }}
of gross issuance against a pre-registered materiality bar of
{{ h.H5.materiality_bar.threshold | pct(0) }}. Over the window NEAR burned
{{ h.H5.totals.burned_near | num }} against net issuance of
{{ h.H5.totals.net_issuance_near | num }} — burn offsets
{{ h.H5.totals.burn_as_pct_of_net_issuance | pct_direct }} of it. Net issuance
was positive in every month.

**4. No primary source documents any Intents fee reaching NEAR holders.**
We checked the Intents fees page, the 1Click fee configuration, the FAQs, the
authentication page and the NEAR-token page. The protocol fee goes to a fee
collector; 1Click keeps its share; the near-intents.org front end routes its
fee to a DAO account. None of them describes a mechanism by which any portion
is burned, staked, or paid to NEAR holders. We pre-committed, in writing and
before looking, to treating a null here as evidence against.

**5. Our bot filter does not catch every bot.**
In {{ m.diagnostics.bot_filter_residual_automation.month }},
{{ m.diagnostics.bot_filter_residual_automation.economic_accounts | num }}
accounts that cleared the filter sent
{{ m.diagnostics.bot_filter_residual_automation.economic_transactions | num }}
transactions between them — a mean of
{{ m.diagnostics.bot_filter_residual_automation.mean_tx_per_account | num }}
against a median of
{{ m.diagnostics.bot_filter_residual_automation.median_tx_per_account | num }}.
That distribution is not a population of humans.

